  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nDry and warm conditions to continue across the forecast area through \nthe day Friday, with low humidity today and tomorrow causing \nelevated fire danger. Humidity and the chances for showers with a \nweak upper-level shortwave or two increase into Saturday and \ncontinue into Sunday. The biggest messaging change with latest \nguidance, is the lower of thunder chances as wave looks weaker and \nless organized than in previous runs of model guidance as the \nbroader synoptic scale features to our west are sampled. The global \nmodels are still struggling to resolve the timing of the shortwave \nand whether it is consolidated or split. The trends however are for \nlowering thunderstorm chances, though slight chance retained \nalong/south of I-20 with this forecast issuance. POPs in general are \ngreatest (50-70% chances) after sunset on Saturday - the more \nfavorable time window for any moderate to heavier showers as well. \nWith Halloween activities, just keep an eye on the forecast and \nconsider rain will be possible.\n\nA return of shortwave ridging for Monday pauses rain chances.  The \npattern remains difficult to draw many explicit solutions from going \ninto the rest of the week. Split flow with active southern and \nnorthern jet stream energy to our west (originating over the Pacific \nand crossing the Rocky Mountains) means that timing and phasing of \nwaves will have a lot of impact on the downstream evolution of low \npressure systems in the central CONUS. The big picture is for a \nprogressive pattern with a deep trough crossing the Rockies Monday \ninto Tuesday, however individual model solutions handle the \ndevelopment of this feature differently between the northern and \nsouthern streams. Generally expect warm, moist return flow from the \nsouth in advance of the system. Some instability is likely to be \navailable for storm development in advance of the wave, but hesitate \nto offer any specific scenario beyond \"rain chances increase \nareawide Tuesday into Wednesday\". /NF/",
